  data:  # cd3a
    kind: work_item  # cd3a
    title: "rust-analyzer/SCIP arm changes zero call edges on a minimal crate while adding duplicate method-kind symbols and 16 origin_run_id validator violations"  # cd3a
    status: todo_hard  # cd3a
    priority: 2  # cd3a
    parent:  # cd3a
    tags:  # cd3a
    - multi_fidelity  # cd3a
    - rust  # cd3a
    - analysis_quality  # cd3a
    - receiver_typing  # cd3a
    isbefore: []  # cd3a
    duplicate_of: []  # cd3a
    not_duplicate_of: []  # cd3a
    pr_ref:  # cd3a
    description: "MEASURED 2026-08-23. Authored two-arm fixture (no build.rs and no dependencies,\nso arm B never executes untrusted code):\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/rust_fidelity_probe_08232026/ -- carries both survey\nartifacts, both verify-claims logs, and the arm-to-arm edge diff.\n\nTHE QUESTION. hypergumbo's declared architecture is multi-fidelity analysis\nwith coexistence (ADR-0012, spec s20), and the rust-analyzer/SCIP backend is\nthe SHIPPED precedent every later backend is argued from. So: when the\nhigher-fidelity backend is enabled, what changes?\n\nPROOF BOTH ARMS ARE REAL, before any claim about the result. Arm A printed\n\"rust-analyzer is installed but NOT enabled\"; arm B printed \"rust-analyzer\nbackend ACTIVE for this run\" and produced 10 nodes carrying origin=['scip']\nthat arm A does not have, plus 16 validation violations arm A does not emit.\nThis is a genuine two-arm comparison, not a silent graceful-degrade.\n\nRESULT: ZERO CALL-FAMILY EDGES DIFFER BETWEEN THE ARMS. Full arm-to-arm edge\ndiff is 13 lines and contains no call edge on either side. SCIP's entire\ncontribution to this fixture is:\n  * 6 `local` nodes and 6 `references` edges pointing at them;\n  * 1 `namespace` node for the crate;\n  * 3 `method`-kind nodes duplicating functions tree-sitter already emitted;\n  * 3 `contains` edges to those duplicates.\nIt resolved no call target and typed no receiver. The one untyped receiver in\nthe fixture (rust:external:0-0:write_all:external_symbol) is untyped in BOTH\narms.\n\nTHE DUPLICATE SYMBOLS ARE A SECOND DEFECT, and they defeat the dedup the\nbackend's own module docstring promises. analyzer.py states \"Stable-id parity\nis already threaded inside translate_scip_to_hg, so the Symbol objects this\nanalyzer emits are deduplicable against the tree-sitter rust.py analyzer's\noutput when cross-pass dedup runs (WI-bajuz)\". Observed:\n\n    rust:src/lib.rs:11-13:load_config:function     <- tree-sitter\n    rust:src/lib.rs:11-11:load_config:method       <- scip\n\nDifferent KIND slot and different SPAN, so no dedup key can match them. All\nthree functions duplicate this way. Node count goes 16 -> 26 for a 3-function\ncrate.\n\nTHIRD: 16 cross_field validation violations, all Edge.origin_run_id failing\n\"must match an AnalysisRun.execution_id in this artifact's analysis_runs\".\nArm A emits zero. So enabling the backend produces a artifact that fails the\nproject's own validator.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT LICENSE. It does NOT show the SCIP backend cannot\nresolve call targets in general -- this fixture is 3 functions with no\ndependencies, and std may not be in the SCIP index without indexing deps at\nall. It DOES show that on the smallest realistic case the shipped precedent\nadds duplicate symbols and validator violations while changing no call edge and\nno verdict, which is a weaker precedent than WI-nanom's description assumes\nwhen it argues the python backend from it.\n\nMEASURE BEFORE BELIEVING: a real multi-crate workspace with indexed\ndependencies, counting call edges gained and receivers typed, arm A vs arm B.\nUntil that exists, \"already shipped for rust\" should not be cited as evidence\nthat a fidelity backend converts into resolved call targets."  # cd3a
    fields: {}  # cd3a
- op: discuss  # dd1d
  at: '2026-08-23T17:49:18Z'  # dd1d
  by: agent  # dd1d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # dd1d
  clock: 2  # dd1d
  nonce: dd1d  # dd1d
  message: "SELF-CORRECTION, filed within the hour, after reading WI-gojum -- which I had\nnot read when filing. Two of this item's claims were framed too strongly. The\nmeasurements stand; two of the readings do not.\n\n1) THE DUPLICATE SYMBOLS ARE NOT A BROKEN PROMISE. I wrote that they \"defeat\nthe dedup the backend's own module docstring promises\". WI-gojum records the\nactual design: the SCIP backend runs ALONGSIDE tree-sitter BY DESIGN\n(ADR-0012 coexistence, Steps 2-3 explicitly parked 2026-07-19 by the owner),\nand \"stable_id parity is readiness for a future dedup, NOT a dedup that runs\ntoday\". The docstring's claim is conditional (\"deduplicable ... WHEN cross-pass\ndedup runs\") and no such pass runs. So duplicate symbols in a coexistence run\nare EXPECTED, not a defect.\n\nWHAT SURVIVES, narrower and worth keeping: the parity is offered as READINESS,\nand on this fixture the keys do not line up for a future dedup to use --\nrust:src/lib.rs:11-13:load_config:function vs\nrust:src/lib.rs:11-11:load_config:method differ in BOTH the kind slot and the\nspan, so no dedup key over (path, span, name, kind) could match them. That is a\nreadiness observation about a parked design, at P3 urgency, not the P2 defect\nthis item's title implies.\n\n2) THE ZERO-CALL-EDGE RESULT IS FIXTURE-SCALE-DEPENDENT, and there is a\ndatapoint that says so which I did not cite. WI-gojum records that an\nalready-merged fix (commit 9266762d4b) to dangling SCIP edge endpoints moved\nzoxide from 0 to 739 scip edges. So the backend demonstrably produces call\nedges at real-crate scale. My \"zero call-family edges differ between arms\" is a\ntrue statement about a 3-function, zero-dependency crate and must not be read\nas a statement about the backend. My own \"WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT LICENSE\"\nparagraph said this; citing zoxide makes it concrete instead of hypothetical.\n\nWHAT IS UNAFFECTED BY BOTH CORRECTIONS:\n  * the 16 Edge.origin_run_id cross_field violations present in arm B and\n    absent in arm A -- enabling the backend produces an artifact that fails the\n    project's own validator. That is a real defect and nothing in WI-gojum\n    covers it.\n  * the verdict result: both arms returned identical verdicts, and the verdict\n    surface carries no fidelity information at all.\n  * the caution against citing \"already shipped for rust\" as evidence that a\n    fidelity backend converts into TYPED RECEIVERS. zoxide's 739 edges show\n    edges are produced; they do not show receivers are typed, which is the\n    property WI-nanom is argued from. That measurement still does not exist.\n\nSUGGESTED RESCOPE for whoever picks this up: drop the dedup half to WI-gojum\n(where the parked design lives), keep the origin_run_id violations as this\nitem, and treat the arm-vs-arm call-edge question as UNMEASURED at realistic\nscale rather than answered."  # dd1d
- op: discuss  # a55e
  at: '2026-09-17T19:45:41Z'  # a55e
  by: agent  # a55e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a55e
  clock: 3  # a55e
  nonce: a55e  # a55e
  message: "RE-MEASURED 2026-09-17 at 06699b1d91, on a real crate instead of the 3-function\nfixture. The defect is far larger than \"16 violations\", and the backend is\nPRODUCTIVE, which makes it worse rather than better.\n\nSETUP: HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER=1 hypergumbo survey ~/whole_bunch_of_repos/aardvark-dns\n(16 .rs files, Rust-only). Backend confirmed active: \"rust-analyzer backend\nACTIVE for this run\" on stderr.\n\nTHE BACKEND WORKS, AND OUT-PRODUCES TREE-SITTER:\n\n    edges origin=['scip']  637      <- the largest single origin in the artifact\n    edges origin=['rust']  445      <- tree-sitter\n    containment-linker     315\n    bash                   118\n\nSo the \"zero call edges differ\" result on the minimal fixture was a property of\nthe FIXTURE (3 functions, no dependencies), not of the backend. This row's own\n\"WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT LICENSE\" paragraph anticipated that; it is now\nsettled in the direction the paragraph reserved.\n\nTHE origin_run_id DEFECT SCALES 1:1 WITH PRODUCTIVITY:\n\n    edges with origin=['scip']                    637\n    edges whose origin_run_id matches no run      637   (ALL of them)\n    total cross_field validation violations      1308\n    sample dangling id     uuid:57da0f03-2c6f-4697-af92-8c8c2e890dbc\n\n16 -> 1308 on a 16-file crate. Every SCIP edge is dangling, not a subset.\n\nROOT CAUSE, and it is one line. The success path is\n\n    return AnalysisResult(symbols=symbols, edges=edges)\n\nwith no run= argument, so AnalysisResult.run defaults to None. The edges carry\nan origin_run_id minted somewhere upstream that no AnalysisRun ever claims.\n\nTHE SECOND CONSEQUENCE, WHICH IS NOT YET FILED ANYWHERE: on the success path\nthe PASS ITSELF DISAPPEARS FROM THE CATALOG. Measured on this artifact,\n`rust_analyzer` is in NEITHER analysis_runs NOR limits.skipped_passes:\n\n    analysis_runs 74 + skipped_passes 84 = 158 passes; rust_analyzer in none\n    pass ids containing \"rust\": runs ['rust', 'rust-trait-dispatch-linker'], skips []\n\nThat breaks the WI-didil completeness contract (\"every registered analyzer\nresolves to exactly one of an AR or a skip record\") specifically WHEN THE\nBACKEND SUCCEEDS. collect_analyzer_result's run-is-None branch only records a\nskip when produced_nothing is true; a producer that emitted output without a\nrun is drained and then silently dropped from the accounting -- which the\nbranch's own comment describes as \"a distinct anomaly\" and deliberately does\nnot record.\n\nSo the artifact says: 637 edges exist, produced by a pass that does not appear\nto have run, attributed to a run that does not exist.\n\nRELATION TO THE OTHER ROWS. WI-zabus names the symbol half of the join at P3;\nthis is the edge half and it is 637 wide. Attaching a real AnalysisRun to the\nsuccess path fixes the dangling join, the completeness violation and WI-zabus\nin one change, and it is the same one-line shape."  # a55e
